1
00:00:32,616 --> 00:00:35,870
The first gateway
to becoming the best K-chef.

2
00:00:38,038 --> 00:00:41,876
120 TEAMS
480 CHEFS

3
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:44,879
WHAT WILL BE THE FIRST DISH
TO DETERMINE THE BEST?

4
00:00:44,962 --> 00:00:46,630
WHAT IS YOUR K-FOOD?

5
00:00:46,714 --> 00:00:48,466
THE TEAMS HAVE 120 MINUTES

6
00:01:06,609 --> 00:01:08,360
One single dish.

7
00:01:11,447 --> 00:01:14,533
For that one perfect dish.

8
00:01:15,493 --> 00:01:19,038
The final eight teams
to advance to the main competition.

9
00:01:27,922 --> 00:01:30,299
The Table: K-Chef 2026.

10
00:01:30,382 --> 00:01:34,512
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026
THE BEGINNING

11
00:01:43,187 --> 00:01:44,271
Welcome, chefs.

12
00:01:44,355 --> 00:01:47,733
You braved the preliminaries
to reach the main competition.

13
00:01:48,317 --> 00:01:51,904
And welcome to the judges
who will accompany them on the journey.

14
00:01:54,949 --> 00:01:58,160
I am your host for The Table: K-Chef 2026

15
00:01:58,244 --> 00:01:59,870
and a K-food ambassador…

16
00:02:01,413 --> 00:02:03,082
Captain Cook!

17
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:15,052
The preliminary rounds
were more heated than ever.

18
00:02:15,135 --> 00:02:17,179
Which teams would advance
to the main competition,

19
00:02:17,263 --> 00:02:19,014
and which teams would drop by the wayside?

20
00:02:19,098 --> 00:02:23,060
The suspense never let up,
and I didn't dare avert my eyes.

21
00:02:23,143 --> 00:02:25,312
To the chefs who presented
the beautiful dishes,

22
00:02:25,396 --> 00:02:28,858
I'd like to take this opportunity
to thank you once again.

23
00:02:31,026 --> 00:02:33,529
Many teams were unfortunately eliminated,

24
00:02:33,612 --> 00:02:36,282
while eight lucky teams advanced.

25
00:02:36,365 --> 00:02:40,619
Regardless, their love and passion
for K-food are second to none.

26
00:02:40,703 --> 00:02:43,664
Here, we have
our eight remaining competitors,

27
00:02:43,747 --> 00:02:47,376
and the audience's interest in them
is higher than ever.

28
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:50,880
What amazing dish will they come up with

29
00:02:50,963 --> 00:02:54,049
in the upcoming rounds
of the main competition?

30
00:02:54,133 --> 00:02:56,844
My mouth is already watering,
just thinking about it.

31
00:02:59,263 --> 00:03:00,347
Now,

32
00:03:00,431 --> 00:03:03,475
we'll take a closer look
at the eight advancing head chefs

33
00:03:03,559 --> 00:03:05,311
in a short while.

34
00:03:05,394 --> 00:03:07,730
Every one of them is skilled and capable,

35
00:03:07,813 --> 00:03:10,274
and I can't wait to see
what they'll create.

36
00:03:11,692 --> 00:03:15,613
Shall we get this exciting show
officially started then?

37
00:03:23,495 --> 00:03:24,538
THEME & MISSION

38
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:31,962
The main competition
is a completely different format

39
00:03:32,046 --> 00:03:33,964
from the previous preliminary rounds.

40
00:03:34,048 --> 00:03:36,175
Unlike the preliminaries,

41
00:03:36,258 --> 00:03:39,595
the food will be judged
by members of the general public.

42
00:03:39,678 --> 00:03:40,930
The three expert judges

43
00:03:41,013 --> 00:03:44,350
won't be the only ones
to taste and judge your food.

44
00:03:44,433 --> 00:03:47,269
Members of the public
connected to the round's theme

45
00:03:47,353 --> 00:03:49,521
will taste and evaluate your food.

46
00:03:49,605 --> 00:03:51,190
One week before the main competition,

47
00:03:51,273 --> 00:03:54,485
we will provide you with a theme.

48
00:03:54,568 --> 00:03:57,112
The theme for the first main round
that happens next week

49
00:03:57,196 --> 00:03:58,197
will be revealed today.

50
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:03,035
You'll prepare for the round
based on that theme.

51
00:04:03,118 --> 00:04:04,745
From the ingredients to recipes,

52
00:04:04,828 --> 00:04:08,666
we'll be looking at
how creatively you interpreted the theme

53
00:04:08,749 --> 00:04:09,917
and prepared for it.

54
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,126
On the day of the competition,

55
00:04:11,210 --> 00:04:14,296
you'll receive a specific challenge
based on that theme.

56
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:19,426
As announced, you may bring
your own secret sauces, broths, and jang,

57
00:04:19,510 --> 00:04:23,806
but the main ingredients must come from
the pantry we set up behind the stage.

58
00:04:23,889 --> 00:04:26,183
Also, the use of monosodium glutamate

59
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:27,685
is not allowed.

60
00:04:30,270 --> 00:04:32,064
You must feel lost.

61
00:04:33,524 --> 00:04:34,942
That is why

62
00:04:36,110 --> 00:04:39,321
instead of being long-winded,
I will announce

63
00:04:39,405 --> 00:04:41,907
the theme for the first round
of the main competition.

64
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:43,534
Here it is!

65
00:04:46,829 --> 00:04:48,497
ROUND 1
ISLAND

66
00:04:57,423 --> 00:04:59,675
THERE IS AN ISLAND BETWEEN PEOPLE
I WANT TO GO TO THAT ISLAND

67
00:05:11,562 --> 00:05:13,605
THERE IS AN ISLAND BETWEEN PEOPLE
I WANT TO GO TO THAT ISLAND

68
00:05:40,424 --> 00:05:41,300
Long time no see.

69
00:05:43,343 --> 00:05:45,971
JANG OK-SEON
OWNER-CHEF OF ONGOJISIM

70
00:05:55,355 --> 00:05:56,190
Yeah.

71
00:05:56,982 --> 00:05:57,858
Six years, is it?

72
00:05:59,485 --> 00:06:01,028
You look well.

73
00:06:02,029 --> 00:06:05,115
Oh, I heard you opened a new restaurant.

74
00:06:05,741 --> 00:06:07,826
-I haven't had a chance to visit.
-Don't worry about it.

75
00:06:08,535 --> 00:06:10,120
You've got enough on your plate.

76
00:06:12,247 --> 00:06:13,165
Right.

77
00:06:15,250 --> 00:06:16,585
This looks bad on my part.

78
00:06:16,668 --> 00:06:18,629
We used to be competitors and rivals,

79
00:06:18,712 --> 00:06:20,881
but now I'm the one being judged.

80
00:06:21,757 --> 00:06:24,384
I guess even this misfortune must be fate.

81
00:06:25,219 --> 00:06:26,095
Ok-seon.

82
00:06:26,178 --> 00:06:27,096
Well then…

83
00:06:28,013 --> 00:06:28,847
see you around

84
00:06:33,352 --> 00:06:35,354
Isn't that too vague a theme?

85
00:06:36,355 --> 00:06:40,359
With a theme so abstract,
won't it be harder to judge the chefs?

86
00:06:42,820 --> 00:06:44,822
Any team that's made it this far

87
00:06:44,905 --> 00:06:48,117
would have the best knowledge and skills
when it comes to cooking.

88
00:06:48,659 --> 00:06:49,785
I think

89
00:06:49,868 --> 00:06:53,163
creativity and understanding
of the Korean sentiment

90
00:06:53,247 --> 00:06:55,749
matter more than technique
in the main competition.

91
00:06:55,833 --> 00:06:57,709
I'd like you to appreciate our intention

92
00:06:57,793 --> 00:07:00,963
to test the chefs' artistic qualities.

93
00:07:03,298 --> 00:07:06,510
I'm aware that Ms. Seon
is an excellent producer.

94
00:07:09,638 --> 00:07:12,015
But this isn't
just an entertaining reality show

95
00:07:12,099 --> 00:07:14,017
where ratings are all that matter.

96
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:17,354
We have to create something
no one's ever seen before.

97
00:07:18,230 --> 00:07:19,064
Don't forget that.

98
00:07:22,609 --> 00:07:23,652
Maybe this is

99
00:07:25,070 --> 00:07:28,866
the vision we've been trying
to bring to life for so long.

100
00:07:31,910 --> 00:07:33,662
THE KOREAN JANG

101
00:07:33,745 --> 00:07:34,663
ISLAND

102
00:07:47,718 --> 00:07:49,928
JOSHUA YEONG-HO PAYTON
OWNER-CHEF OF HUSH

103
00:07:57,311 --> 00:07:59,730
ISABEL DOMINGUEZ
HEAD CHEF OF INTEGRAL

104
00:08:09,990 --> 00:08:11,116
Oh, boy.

105
00:08:12,117 --> 00:08:13,202
"Island"?

106
00:08:13,285 --> 00:08:15,412
What else would you get from an island?

107
00:08:15,495 --> 00:08:17,456
All this fish comes from the sea.

108
00:08:17,539 --> 00:08:20,375
Right. I was curious, that's all.

109
00:08:20,459 --> 00:08:24,630
I was wondering if there was
a special ingredient from an island.

110
00:08:25,422 --> 00:08:27,549
Well, nothing really comes to mind.

111
00:08:27,633 --> 00:08:31,136
Unless you mean
fermented skate from Heuksando.

112
00:08:35,432 --> 00:08:37,392
POETS OF OUR TIME

113
00:08:40,145 --> 00:08:42,272
"A symphony of light and shadows."

114
00:08:42,356 --> 00:08:43,941
"The life of a human being

115
00:08:44,024 --> 00:08:46,526
is a long journey
that spans the whole spectrum."

116
00:08:46,610 --> 00:08:48,862
"Light symbolizes freedom and hope,

117
00:08:48,946 --> 00:08:53,158
and shadows are by-products of the effort
that comes from withstanding that."

118
00:08:54,117 --> 00:08:55,702
"Solitude, fear,

119
00:08:56,453 --> 00:08:58,288
despair, anxiety."

120
00:09:05,504 --> 00:09:07,714
"Humans take ownership of their lives
by making decisions

121
00:09:07,798 --> 00:09:09,508
while enduring the eternal punishment
of paying the price."

122
00:09:09,591 --> 00:09:12,052
"They try to form
a meaningful relationship with others,

123
00:09:12,135 --> 00:09:14,429
but love and understanding
aren't the only things that are made."

124
00:09:14,513 --> 00:09:16,598
"Communication always comes
with self-denial and doubt

125
00:09:16,682 --> 00:09:20,060
so we're individuals in a crowd
that must accept the bad with the good."

126
00:09:22,854 --> 00:09:26,149
THERE IS AN ISLAND BETWEEN PEOPLE
I WANT TO GO TO THAT ISLAND

127
00:10:30,505 --> 00:10:32,257
What could that island be?

128
00:10:35,093 --> 00:10:39,181
Do they want us to make something
with an ingredient

129
00:10:39,264 --> 00:10:41,767
from a specific island?

130
00:10:42,601 --> 00:10:46,188
Something strange or unique,
that's hard to find?

131
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:50,442
That's exactly it.

132
00:10:50,525 --> 00:10:54,363
You must create the ultimate dish
with marine algae

133
00:10:54,446 --> 00:10:57,908
that grows on the southernmost island
of Marado, Korea.

134
00:10:57,991 --> 00:10:59,576
No, no.

135
00:10:59,659 --> 00:11:03,121
There's no way
it's that simple of a mission.

136
00:11:06,666 --> 00:11:07,584
Could it be

137
00:11:08,418 --> 00:11:12,589
that they want us to plate it up
in a way that evokes an island?

138
00:11:15,884 --> 00:11:16,927
Correct!

139
00:11:17,010 --> 00:11:19,888
You put together a plate
that looks most like an island.

140
00:11:19,971 --> 00:11:21,556
You are the winner!

141
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:23,183
No.

142
00:11:23,266 --> 00:11:25,477
It must be something hard to imagine.

143
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:27,729
Something most people wouldn't think of.

144
00:11:28,230 --> 00:11:30,023
That's what it must be.

145
00:11:35,779 --> 00:11:36,947
Could it be…

146
00:11:37,989 --> 00:11:39,658
At last, you've figured it out.

147
00:11:45,288 --> 00:11:46,123
That's right.

148
00:11:46,206 --> 00:11:48,291
The island is just a metaphor.

149
00:11:48,375 --> 00:11:49,876
It's a figure of speech.

150
00:11:52,087 --> 00:11:53,880
So that's what it is?

151
00:11:55,090 --> 00:11:55,966
Wait.

152
00:11:57,217 --> 00:12:00,220
What does
"there's an island between people" mean?

153
00:12:00,804 --> 00:12:04,683
Islands exist in the ocean,
not between people.

154
00:12:07,185 --> 00:12:08,437
Excuse me.

155
00:12:09,604 --> 00:12:12,065
The VIP's main course is ready.

156
00:12:12,149 --> 00:12:12,983
What?

157
00:12:14,776 --> 00:12:15,944
Huh?

158
00:12:16,027 --> 00:12:19,156
The VIP's main course is ready, Chef.

159
00:12:20,449 --> 00:12:22,242
Okay. Good.

160
00:12:24,995 --> 00:12:26,204
Shall I serve them?

161
00:12:27,497 --> 00:12:29,749
No. I'll serve them myself.

162
00:12:52,564 --> 00:12:55,317
THE STRONGEST HOPE
IS BORN FROM THE GREATEST DESPAIR!

163
00:13:17,589 --> 00:13:20,175
THE ISLANDS OF KOREA

164
00:13:36,733 --> 00:13:37,609
Chris.

165
00:13:38,735 --> 00:13:40,237
Were you here all night?

166
00:13:42,405 --> 00:13:44,741
Why didn't you sleep at all?

167
00:13:46,493 --> 00:13:47,911
I took it too lightly.

168
00:13:48,495 --> 00:13:50,080
I never imagined it would be like this.

169
00:13:51,164 --> 00:13:54,334
This is just the first main round.
I didn't think it would be this tough.

170
00:13:57,045 --> 00:13:59,589
This is tough.

171
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:04,386
WANTING TO GO TO AN ISLAND?

172
00:14:04,469 --> 00:14:05,387
"Island."

173
00:14:06,805 --> 00:14:07,639
Island.

174
00:14:10,392 --> 00:14:12,060
"There's an island between people."

175
00:14:12,769 --> 00:14:14,104
"I want to go to that island."

176
00:14:14,980 --> 00:14:16,356
There's an island.

177
00:14:18,942 --> 00:14:20,902
I want to go toward that island.

178
00:14:21,486 --> 00:14:22,320
Why?

179
00:14:26,908 --> 00:14:27,742
Chris.

180
00:14:28,451 --> 00:14:31,788
When did you leave Korea?
What grade were you in?

181
00:14:34,291 --> 00:14:35,584
I was in third grade.

182
00:14:35,667 --> 00:14:36,960
I left after finishing third grade.

183
00:14:37,544 --> 00:14:38,837
You left young.

184
00:14:40,463 --> 00:14:43,758
I bet it threw you off when they gave us
a Korean poem as the mission.

185
00:14:43,842 --> 00:14:46,886
But I don't know the poem
that well either.

186
00:14:46,970 --> 00:14:50,098
I'm pretty sure it was the same
for the other chefs.

187
00:14:57,439 --> 00:14:59,608
There must be a reason
they chose that poem.

188
00:15:03,820 --> 00:15:05,405
Ingredients from an island.

189
00:15:05,488 --> 00:15:06,906
It can't be that easy.

190
00:15:10,660 --> 00:15:12,662
It has to be a metaphor.

191
00:15:31,514 --> 00:15:32,349
Let's go outside.

192
00:15:35,185 --> 00:15:36,811
Let's go outside and take a stroll.

193
00:15:37,437 --> 00:15:39,230
We can go to a park.

194
00:15:40,649 --> 00:15:41,483
A park?

195
00:15:41,566 --> 00:15:42,442
Yes.

196
00:15:42,525 --> 00:15:45,862
Let's go to a big, open space
and get some fresh air.

197
00:15:46,404 --> 00:15:47,572
Let's slack off.

198
00:15:49,199 --> 00:15:51,242
What I mean is

199
00:15:51,326 --> 00:15:54,746
we can relax for a few hours and goof off.

200
00:15:56,122 --> 00:15:57,207
I'll get changed.

201
00:16:01,836 --> 00:16:02,712
"Slack off"?

202
00:16:04,089 --> 00:16:05,006
"Goof off"?

203
00:16:28,196 --> 00:16:29,656
They look nice and fresh.

204
00:16:30,281 --> 00:16:33,368
Did you gather them yourself from nearby?

205
00:16:33,451 --> 00:16:34,911
Yes, of course.

206
00:16:34,994 --> 00:16:37,163
My husband went out

207
00:16:37,247 --> 00:16:40,542
early this morning
and caught them all by hand.

208
00:16:41,710 --> 00:16:43,461
What do you think?
Aren't the colors so pretty?

209
00:16:43,545 --> 00:16:44,379
They are.

210
00:16:45,880 --> 00:16:47,006
They look delicious.

211
00:16:47,090 --> 00:16:49,718
Why don't you buy some then?

212
00:16:49,801 --> 00:16:50,885
Just 3,000 won for a bag.

213
00:16:51,553 --> 00:16:52,512
Gosh.

214
00:16:53,388 --> 00:16:54,556
That's so cheap.

215
00:16:55,640 --> 00:16:58,393
Or shall I boil some for you right now?

216
00:17:45,815 --> 00:17:47,275
CHEF CHA

217
00:17:49,110 --> 00:17:50,111
Where did you go?

218
00:17:50,653 --> 00:17:53,656
I heard you took off somewhere
after dinner service last night.

219
00:17:54,365 --> 00:17:55,492
I just thought

220
00:17:55,575 --> 00:17:57,243
maybe the sea breeze

221
00:17:59,037 --> 00:18:00,413
would give me an idea.

222
00:18:00,997 --> 00:18:04,751
Though of course,
you wouldn't have made it that easy.

223
00:18:07,754 --> 00:18:08,671
The thing is, though…

224
00:18:12,133 --> 00:18:13,051
Aren't we all…

225
00:18:15,220 --> 00:18:16,054
an island of sorts?

226
00:18:17,847 --> 00:18:19,224
An island

227
00:18:19,808 --> 00:18:20,642
doesn't have to be

228
00:18:22,268 --> 00:18:24,270
floating in the middle of the sea.

229
00:18:25,230 --> 00:18:26,898
What if someone wants to go to an island,

230
00:18:27,816 --> 00:18:29,692
but they're unable to?

231
00:18:30,860 --> 00:18:32,946
You can still help them get there, right?

232
00:19:10,316 --> 00:19:12,777
Chris Krantz…

233
00:19:12,861 --> 00:19:14,279
Werst…

234
00:19:14,362 --> 00:19:16,281
What a tongue-twister of a name.

235
00:19:16,364 --> 00:19:18,741
The bottom line is,
people call him Chris K.

236
00:19:19,534 --> 00:19:23,246
His dad is German-American,
and his mom died when he was young.

237
00:19:23,329 --> 00:19:26,541
When was he adopted then?
When did he leave Korea?

238
00:19:26,624 --> 00:19:31,129
His file says his American father
adopted him when he was 14.

239
00:19:31,713 --> 00:19:32,964
Where was he before that?

240
00:19:33,047 --> 00:19:35,216
That's what I can't figure out.

241
00:19:36,009 --> 00:19:38,219
There's no record of him
going to school until eighth grade,

242
00:19:38,303 --> 00:19:40,889
no registered addresses,
and no family on file either.

243
00:19:40,972 --> 00:19:43,391
What on earth does that mean?

244
00:19:43,474 --> 00:19:46,352
Did he come from outer space or something?

245
00:19:47,061 --> 00:19:48,062
I'll look into that too.

246
00:19:48,646 --> 00:19:54,193
Even his colleagues say
they know nothing about Chris K.'s past.

247
00:19:54,277 --> 00:19:55,278
His American father.

248
00:19:56,321 --> 00:19:59,908
We're trying to locate
his American father.

249
00:19:59,991 --> 00:20:00,825
His American father?

250
00:20:01,701 --> 00:20:03,328
But even that's proving difficult.

251
00:20:04,579 --> 00:20:07,540
There's talk that he moved
from the US to Germany…

252
00:20:07,624 --> 00:20:10,793
Why is it so hard
to find out about one man?

253
00:20:10,877 --> 00:20:14,130
Is this the best Pyeongyeon Group can do?

254
00:20:14,714 --> 00:20:15,548
I apologize.

255
00:20:16,507 --> 00:20:19,552
Call our German office and get them
to look into the adoptive dad.

256
00:20:19,636 --> 00:20:22,180
Not just about him, but his whole family.

257
00:20:22,263 --> 00:20:24,599
Tell them to find out whatever they can.

258
00:20:25,099 --> 00:20:26,142
Yes, ma'am.

259
00:20:26,851 --> 00:20:27,727
Then again,

260
00:20:28,519 --> 00:20:31,564
it doesn't matter
what he used to be like in the past.

261
00:20:32,565 --> 00:20:35,151
What matters is how skilled he is now.

262
00:20:35,234 --> 00:20:36,653
That's what I need to know.

263
00:20:36,736 --> 00:20:37,904
What else?

264
00:20:37,987 --> 00:20:41,324
His connection to Famiglia.
That's extremely important.

265
00:20:46,871 --> 00:20:47,956
It's hot!

266
00:20:48,581 --> 00:20:50,500
Help! I need help!

267
00:20:54,712 --> 00:20:56,339
"Was it in a furnace? Why is it so hot?"

268
00:20:56,422 --> 00:20:58,174
-Yes!
-It's hot coffee…

269
00:20:58,257 --> 00:20:59,425
Are you all right?

270
00:20:59,509 --> 00:21:01,386
Wait. Why does it taste wrong?

271
00:21:01,469 --> 00:21:03,930
There's no aroma and it's too acidic.

272
00:21:04,013 --> 00:21:07,016
Are you sure it's from Guatemala?
Who brewed the coffee today?

273
00:21:07,100 --> 00:21:08,643
Jeong took today off.

274
00:21:08,726 --> 00:21:10,561
I'll instruct his temporary replacement.

275
00:21:10,645 --> 00:21:11,729
Darn it!

276
00:21:11,813 --> 00:21:13,439
-Are you okay?
-I don't know!

277
00:21:14,983 --> 00:21:15,900
It's still hot.

278
00:21:16,859 --> 00:21:18,111
Ms. Baek.

279
00:21:18,194 --> 00:21:19,028
What?

280
00:21:19,779 --> 00:21:21,155
There's one thing that bothers me.

281
00:21:22,824 --> 00:21:23,700
What is it?

282
00:21:25,034 --> 00:21:26,369
Here.

283
00:21:26,452 --> 00:21:28,413
In one of the competitions mentioned here,

284
00:21:28,496 --> 00:21:30,123
a restaurant Chris K. worked at

285
00:21:30,206 --> 00:21:32,917
and Cha Woo-jin's restaurant
went head-to-head.

286
00:21:33,710 --> 00:21:34,585
Really?

287
00:21:35,378 --> 00:21:37,130
Where? Where does it say that?

288
00:21:37,213 --> 00:21:38,589
Here. It was held in Belgium.

289
00:21:39,382 --> 00:21:40,591
It was a competition in Belgium.

290
00:21:41,801 --> 00:21:43,803
WINNING TEAM AURELIA
& HEAD CHEF JULIAN HAYES

291
00:21:46,806 --> 00:21:47,640
What?

292
00:21:48,766 --> 00:21:50,435
Cha Woo-jin came second?

293
00:21:50,518 --> 00:21:51,436
Yes.

294
00:21:52,812 --> 00:21:53,730
Hold on.

295
00:21:55,023 --> 00:21:57,233
You said Chris K.
worked at this restaurant.

296
00:21:57,316 --> 00:21:58,735
His name isn't on here.

297
00:21:58,818 --> 00:22:00,445
Come on,

298
00:22:00,528 --> 00:22:02,405
I explained that earlier.

299
00:22:02,989 --> 00:22:06,868
Chris K. has an unusual tendency
to work only as a sous-chef.

300
00:22:06,951 --> 00:22:09,746
The sous-chef of the restaurant
that won this competition

301
00:22:09,829 --> 00:22:11,289
was Chris K.

302
00:22:12,707 --> 00:22:15,334
And the winning dish was his creation.

303
00:22:16,544 --> 00:22:19,005
So effectively,

304
00:22:19,797 --> 00:22:21,632
he was the head chef.

305
00:22:22,592 --> 00:22:23,551
What?

306
00:22:23,634 --> 00:22:25,970
The one and only competition

307
00:22:26,971 --> 00:22:29,140
that Cha Woo-jin failed to win.

308
00:22:30,600 --> 00:22:31,642
This is it.

309
00:22:37,356 --> 00:22:38,733
Things are going to get

310
00:22:39,776 --> 00:22:41,652
a little more complicated than I thought.

311
00:22:42,570 --> 00:22:43,946
Famiglia…

312
00:22:44,030 --> 00:22:46,407
A guy like that joined Famiglia,

313
00:22:46,491 --> 00:22:49,827
and he wants to take us on and beat us.

314
00:22:49,911 --> 00:22:52,705
No, he's saying he can beat us.

315
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,795
How interesting.

316
00:23:00,463 --> 00:23:01,589
This is interesting.

317
00:23:21,275 --> 00:23:22,777
Sorry about that.

318
00:23:22,860 --> 00:23:24,028
I must've dozed off.

319
00:23:24,112 --> 00:23:25,363
Don't worry.

320
00:23:25,446 --> 00:23:27,990
You were so fast asleep,
I couldn't wake you up.

321
00:23:28,074 --> 00:23:30,660
I'm glad you got at least some good sleep.

322
00:23:32,620 --> 00:23:33,454
Did I snore?

323
00:23:34,122 --> 00:23:34,956
Yes.

324
00:23:35,039 --> 00:23:37,959
You snored so loud and long.

325
00:23:38,042 --> 00:23:39,752
You must've been really tired.

326
00:23:42,213 --> 00:23:43,631
I must've slept well.

327
00:23:55,726 --> 00:23:57,603
There are way more people than before.

328
00:23:58,771 --> 00:24:00,648
Yes. It's lunchtime.

329
00:24:01,440 --> 00:24:02,984
Yeouido is like this.

330
00:24:03,693 --> 00:24:05,778
There are lots of offices here.

331
00:24:05,862 --> 00:24:07,530
The streets are usually very quiet,

332
00:24:07,613 --> 00:24:10,449
but they come out in force
when it's lunchtime.

333
00:24:12,827 --> 00:24:15,830
They look like they're in uniforms.

334
00:24:16,581 --> 00:24:18,708
Wall Street is a lot like this.

335
00:24:19,792 --> 00:24:21,502
Come to think of it,

336
00:24:22,128 --> 00:24:23,838
this place is an island too, isn't it?

337
00:24:23,921 --> 00:24:25,798
-What?
-Yeouido.

338
00:24:25,882 --> 00:24:29,093
It's a district sitting
in the middle of Hangang River.

339
00:24:29,177 --> 00:24:32,096
It's connected by bridges
to either side of the river.

340
00:24:32,180 --> 00:24:34,140
It's not in the middle of an ocean,

341
00:24:34,223 --> 00:24:37,226
but it's still an island
floating in the river.

342
00:25:05,546 --> 00:25:07,965
Everyone looks so lonely.

343
00:25:30,404 --> 00:25:33,366
That poem we read, Chris.

344
00:25:37,495 --> 00:25:38,329
Chris.

345
00:25:40,373 --> 00:25:41,207
Chris?

346
00:25:44,710 --> 00:25:45,544
Yes?

347
00:25:46,295 --> 00:25:48,547
The poem. Island.

348
00:25:50,466 --> 00:25:51,342
Right.

349
00:25:51,968 --> 00:25:53,636
The second line says

350
00:25:53,719 --> 00:25:56,097
the person wants to go to the island.

351
00:25:56,180 --> 00:25:57,348
When you think about it,

352
00:25:57,431 --> 00:26:00,643
wanting to go somewhere
means they can't actually go.

353
00:26:00,726 --> 00:26:03,396
They want to go, but can't.

354
00:26:05,314 --> 00:26:08,526
Couldn't someone help them get there?

355
00:26:09,110 --> 00:26:11,404
Couldn't someone offer some kind of help?

356
00:26:13,281 --> 00:26:16,367
What if you were that someone, Chris?

357
00:26:25,960 --> 00:26:27,503
THERE IS AN ISLAND BETWEEN PEOPLE
I WANT TO GO TO THAT ISLAND

358
00:26:27,586 --> 00:26:29,588
PARENT-CHILD CONVERSATION TIME

359
00:26:29,672 --> 00:26:31,465
WEEKLY AVERAGE: 68 MINUTES OR LESS

360
00:26:32,675 --> 00:26:33,843
CONVERSATION DURING MEALS: 5 MINUTES

361
00:26:41,559 --> 00:26:44,645
According to a 2026 survey by Opinion Now,

362
00:26:44,729 --> 00:26:47,815
Korean families
with children over 12 years old

363
00:26:47,898 --> 00:26:51,986
talk to each other for less than
68 minutes per week on average.

364
00:26:52,069 --> 00:26:54,363
Of that, only five minutes

365
00:26:54,447 --> 00:26:57,408
happen at the table during mealtimes.

366
00:26:58,617 --> 00:27:01,746
To increase familial communication,
the government recommends

367
00:27:01,829 --> 00:27:03,914
that children be allowed less screen time

368
00:27:03,998 --> 00:27:07,335
and given more incentive
to speak with their parents.

369
00:27:07,418 --> 00:27:11,297
However, that only forces
surface-level talk

370
00:27:11,380 --> 00:27:14,425
and experts voice their doubt
on how effective those measures are.

371
00:27:21,223 --> 00:27:25,311
The Table: K-Chef 2026's solution
to improving communication…

372
00:27:28,064 --> 00:27:30,483
is the dining table.

373
00:27:32,360 --> 00:27:33,277
-What?
-The dining table?

374
00:27:34,153 --> 00:27:35,029
The dining table?

375
00:27:37,239 --> 00:27:40,451
Cameras eight and nine,
focus on Cha Woo-jin and Chris K.

376
00:27:41,118 --> 00:27:42,661
Captain Cook, standby for next comment.

377
00:27:46,123 --> 00:27:47,458
Ready the screen.

378
00:27:47,541 --> 00:27:48,459
Graphics.

379
00:27:49,418 --> 00:27:50,461
In.

380
00:27:51,379 --> 00:27:52,338
SIKGU

381
00:27:52,421 --> 00:27:54,507
Do you know what these characters say?

382
00:27:55,925 --> 00:27:58,594
Yes. It says "sikgu."

383
00:27:59,303 --> 00:28:01,680
What do these characters mean?

384
00:28:02,807 --> 00:28:05,393
They literally mean
"mouths that eat together."

385
00:28:05,976 --> 00:28:09,146
It indicates people
who gather around to eat together.

386
00:28:10,106 --> 00:28:13,734
Look at the shape
of the character on the right.

387
00:28:14,610 --> 00:28:17,071
Doesn't it look like a square table?

388
00:28:18,030 --> 00:28:19,865
It's where we sit, eat,

389
00:28:19,949 --> 00:28:21,867
and gain the energy
to get through the day.

390
00:28:22,952 --> 00:28:24,787
We eat food that provides us with energy,

391
00:28:24,870 --> 00:28:28,582
and as members of a community,
we exchange a great deal.

392
00:28:29,166 --> 00:28:30,918
We celebrate happy occasions,

393
00:28:31,001 --> 00:28:33,754
and offer condolences in times of sorrow.

394
00:28:35,881 --> 00:28:39,427
When conversation stalls,
we take a break and eat good food.

395
00:28:40,261 --> 00:28:41,720
When we run out of things to say,

396
00:28:41,804 --> 00:28:44,598
the food itself can become the topic.

397
00:28:47,601 --> 00:28:50,187
Today, we stress the importance
of communication within families.

398
00:28:50,271 --> 00:28:53,274
However, we're at a loss
as to how to go about it.

399
00:28:53,357 --> 00:28:56,944
Spend more time together
and turn off your phones?

400
00:28:57,611 --> 00:28:59,363
Instead of offering vague strategies,

401
00:28:59,447 --> 00:29:01,907
we'd like to offer a different approach.

402
00:29:06,620 --> 00:29:09,415
By increasing the time
spent at the dining table,

403
00:29:09,498 --> 00:29:12,168
we can encourage communication
between generations.

404
00:29:13,002 --> 00:29:15,671
That is our strategy.

405
00:29:17,798 --> 00:29:20,593
Bring the family together
at the dining table.

406
00:29:20,676 --> 00:29:24,054
Connect the individual islands
that make up a family.

407
00:29:24,805 --> 00:29:26,182
That is the mission

408
00:29:27,099 --> 00:29:29,685
for the first round
of the main competition.

409
00:29:30,269 --> 00:29:31,103
What?

410
00:29:31,645 --> 00:29:32,646
What did he say?

411
00:29:32,730 --> 00:29:33,981
Do what with the dining table?

412
00:29:36,317 --> 00:29:37,651
Connect people?

413
00:29:37,735 --> 00:29:38,861
What does that mean?

414
00:29:48,996 --> 00:29:50,289
In the pantry behind you

415
00:29:50,372 --> 00:29:53,125
are ingredients that Judge Kim Pil-hyeon
personally gathered from coastal rocks,

416
00:29:53,209 --> 00:29:55,753
as well as other ingredients you need.

417
00:29:56,712 --> 00:29:59,423
Choose your ingredients
and head to your assigned kitchen.

418
00:30:00,299 --> 00:30:01,175
You have…

419
00:30:05,304 --> 00:30:06,305
three hours!

420
00:30:11,977 --> 00:30:13,687
-Go!
-Hurry!

421
00:30:34,667 --> 00:30:35,751
Here, take this.

422
00:30:52,685 --> 00:30:53,519
Come in.

423
00:30:59,942 --> 00:31:01,652
How do you think the first round is going?

424
00:31:02,194 --> 00:31:03,112
Well, so far…

425
00:31:03,195 --> 00:31:05,197
Bad? Not bad? Looking okay?

426
00:31:09,368 --> 00:31:12,538
They have three hours to cook something
that fits the mission.

427
00:31:12,621 --> 00:31:14,248
I can't tell what they'll cook.

428
00:31:15,124 --> 00:31:17,501
To be honest, I think it's tough.

429
00:31:18,586 --> 00:31:20,212
-What is?
-The mission.

430
00:31:21,130 --> 00:31:23,007
Something about connecting islands.

431
00:31:24,216 --> 00:31:27,595
They invited six families of four
as guest judges

432
00:31:27,678 --> 00:31:30,556
to taste the food and then score it.

433
00:31:30,639 --> 00:31:33,058
Is that even an effective way
to evaluate food?

434
00:31:33,142 --> 00:31:34,727
I'm not too sure.

435
00:31:34,810 --> 00:31:37,354
We invited ordinary families
as guest judges

436
00:31:38,314 --> 00:31:41,483
to see how they react
to the food they're served,

437
00:31:41,567 --> 00:31:43,152
how much it gets them talking,

438
00:31:43,235 --> 00:31:44,945
and whether it piques their interest.

439
00:31:45,696 --> 00:31:50,075
The objective judging and criticism
will come from the three expert judges.

440
00:31:50,784 --> 00:31:52,911
The two different kinds of scoring methods

441
00:31:52,995 --> 00:31:54,580
should help us decide on a winner.

442
00:31:55,706 --> 00:31:56,540
What?

443
00:31:57,166 --> 00:31:58,542
You already knew all this?

444
00:32:00,336 --> 00:32:03,088
I spoke with Ms. Seon briefly
before filming began.

445
00:32:03,172 --> 00:32:05,924
Why'd you ask to see me
if you don't need an explanation?

446
00:32:18,103 --> 00:32:19,897
I asked to see you

447
00:32:21,106 --> 00:32:22,232
for something else.

448
00:32:27,613 --> 00:32:29,490
I need your help, Ms. Baek.

449
00:32:42,086 --> 00:32:43,921
The carrots are too thick.
Slice them thinner.

450
00:32:44,004 --> 00:32:44,838
Yes, Chef.

451
00:32:56,475 --> 00:33:00,229
Today, we won't judge the dishes
on how complete they are.

452
00:33:00,813 --> 00:33:04,775
We'll judge them based on the reactions
of our guest judges, the families of four.

453
00:33:11,615 --> 00:33:12,658
How long do you need?

454
00:33:12,741 --> 00:33:13,701
I'll be done in a minute.

455
00:33:15,953 --> 00:33:16,995
Okay.

456
00:33:47,776 --> 00:33:49,194
All right!

457
00:34:12,259 --> 00:34:13,427
Rather than cooking technique,

458
00:34:13,510 --> 00:34:17,473
please focus more on creativity
and the interpretation of the mission.

459
00:34:27,900 --> 00:34:29,401
Isn't this doenjang?

460
00:34:30,694 --> 00:34:32,446
I suddenly feel much less enthusiastic.

461
00:34:35,073 --> 00:34:36,074
I mean,

462
00:34:36,742 --> 00:34:39,119
why bring doenjang
to cook French and Italian cuisine?

463
00:34:40,662 --> 00:34:41,538
Chris.

464
00:34:42,039 --> 00:34:44,625
What exactly are you
going to do with this?

465
00:34:45,459 --> 00:34:46,293
What do you think?

466
00:34:47,044 --> 00:34:48,045
We're going to win in style.

467
00:34:52,633 --> 00:34:54,343
It's the traditional kind of doenjang.

468
00:34:54,426 --> 00:34:55,552
It's too pungent.

469
00:34:55,636 --> 00:34:59,139
I don't think it'll work
with Western fine dining.

470
00:34:59,223 --> 00:35:00,474
It's not just any traditional doenjang.

471
00:35:01,600 --> 00:35:03,101
This is priceless stuff.

472
00:35:07,940 --> 00:35:09,233
A bridge that will connect

473
00:35:10,442 --> 00:35:11,568
the islands within a family.

474
00:35:13,529 --> 00:35:14,822
Six families of four,

475
00:35:15,697 --> 00:35:16,824
three main judges.

476
00:35:16,907 --> 00:35:19,368
Nine plates total,
with dishes from eight teams.

477
00:35:20,160 --> 00:35:22,287
Each dish gets about
three to four minutes.

478
00:35:22,996 --> 00:35:25,666
To get people talking
in that short amount of time,

479
00:35:25,749 --> 00:35:28,168
the food itself must become the topic.

480
00:35:29,586 --> 00:35:32,047
Conversation starts
with something familiar,

481
00:35:32,130 --> 00:35:33,632
something that brings back memories.

482
00:35:35,592 --> 00:35:38,428
The dish must tell a story
to keep them from getting bored.

483
00:35:40,597 --> 00:35:42,266
A flashy drama that will keep them

484
00:35:43,141 --> 00:35:44,226
on the edge of their seats.

485
00:35:47,145 --> 00:35:48,814
The key to any drama is tension.

486
00:35:49,398 --> 00:35:51,900
A balance between
familiarity and unfamiliarity.

487
00:36:01,994 --> 00:36:03,036
And the starting point is…

488
00:36:06,707 --> 00:36:08,166
curiosity.

489
00:36:13,964 --> 00:36:14,965
And…

490
00:36:17,968 --> 00:36:19,386
a connection with the audience.

491
00:36:25,934 --> 00:36:29,563
Cha Woo-jin cannot win this competition.

492
00:36:29,646 --> 00:36:30,689
What?

493
00:36:30,772 --> 00:36:31,857
Hold on.

494
00:36:31,940 --> 00:36:34,067
Hold on. What on earth…

495
00:36:34,151 --> 00:36:35,360
What are you saying?

496
00:36:35,444 --> 00:36:37,946
Cha Woo-jin,
who represents Pyeongyeon Group,

497
00:36:38,030 --> 00:36:40,032
cannot win the competition?

498
00:36:40,115 --> 00:36:41,909
Then who should?

499
00:36:45,412 --> 00:36:49,166
Of course, Cha Woo-jin
is a crucial part of the competition.

500
00:36:49,958 --> 00:36:53,086
We need Korea's biggest celebrity chef
to generate buzz.

501
00:36:54,212 --> 00:36:57,883
The longer he lasts in the competition,
the better the ratings will be.

502
00:37:00,344 --> 00:37:01,178
Ms. Baek.

503
00:37:02,054 --> 00:37:06,183
I want this competition
to be Woo-jin's last as a chef.

504
00:37:08,894 --> 00:37:10,479
As soon as the competition ends,

505
00:37:12,189 --> 00:37:14,107
I plan to legally register him as my son.

506
00:37:14,691 --> 00:37:16,360
-What?
-I get it.

507
00:37:16,443 --> 00:37:21,698
Woo-jin did a great deal for us as a chef,
and he will continue to do so.

508
00:37:23,158 --> 00:37:25,285
Of course, we've helped him
a great deal too.

509
00:37:28,205 --> 00:37:29,289
But he…

510
00:37:30,207 --> 00:37:31,708
means a great deal to me as well.

511
00:37:32,334 --> 00:37:35,837
It's possible
that I've been able to last this long

512
00:37:36,338 --> 00:37:38,924
because I had Woo-jin's support.

513
00:37:39,800 --> 00:37:41,677
Ever since we first met,

514
00:37:41,760 --> 00:37:44,096
he's stayed by my side
with unwavering loyalty.

515
00:37:44,763 --> 00:37:46,056
How much longer

516
00:37:46,807 --> 00:37:48,016
can I keep working?

517
00:37:49,017 --> 00:37:50,310
I'm tired now.

518
00:37:50,394 --> 00:37:52,437
Once this competition ends in a success,

519
00:37:52,521 --> 00:37:54,898
I plan to resign as CEO of Pyeongyeon F&B.

520
00:37:55,899 --> 00:37:58,235
Before that,
I need a succession plan in place.

521
00:37:58,318 --> 00:37:59,403
As you know…

522
00:38:03,407 --> 00:38:04,992
I don't have any children.

523
00:38:08,286 --> 00:38:10,539
If the man I were to adopt
and name as my successor

524
00:38:10,622 --> 00:38:13,333
wins our latest cooking competition,

525
00:38:13,417 --> 00:38:16,503
it'll immediately raise questions
about the competition's fairness.

526
00:38:17,295 --> 00:38:19,923
It would also be detrimental
to how the public views Pyeongyeon.

527
00:38:21,508 --> 00:38:25,345
Seon Jeong-won is great at her job,
but she's excessively principled,

528
00:38:25,429 --> 00:38:26,596
almost to a fault.

529
00:38:33,562 --> 00:38:35,439
That's why I need your help.

530
00:38:36,940 --> 00:38:38,567
Do you get what I'm saying?

531
00:38:40,193 --> 00:38:42,070
He can't win.

532
00:38:43,405 --> 00:38:46,575
Cha Woo-jin cannot win.

533
00:38:47,409 --> 00:38:49,286
Because he has to become her successor.

534
00:38:50,495 --> 00:38:53,290
She wants to make
the competition a success

535
00:38:53,373 --> 00:38:54,624
and save the company.

536
00:38:55,292 --> 00:38:57,878
She boosts her public image
by retiring in style,

537
00:38:57,961 --> 00:39:01,548
while leaving a successor
who will do exactly as she wants.

538
00:39:03,175 --> 00:39:05,135
She's either two-faced
or has an ulterior motive.

539
00:39:06,511 --> 00:39:09,306
She's the worst viper I've ever seen.

540
00:39:10,599 --> 00:39:12,476
Is something wrong, Ms. Baek?

541
00:39:12,559 --> 00:39:13,810
It's nothing.

542
00:39:14,686 --> 00:39:17,647
All my hard work
might just benefit someone else.

543
00:39:20,025 --> 00:39:21,443
You're down to your last minute.

544
00:39:21,526 --> 00:39:22,611
One minute to go.

545
00:39:22,694 --> 00:39:25,322
Chefs, please begin wrapping up.

546
00:40:34,683 --> 00:40:36,351
This is the last announcement.

547
00:40:36,434 --> 00:40:38,395
You have ten seconds left.

548
00:40:38,979 --> 00:40:39,813
Ten.

549
00:40:41,273 --> 00:40:42,149
Nine.

550
00:40:43,483 --> 00:40:44,734
Eight.

551
00:40:45,277 --> 00:40:46,111
Seven.

552
00:40:47,112 --> 00:40:47,946
Six.

553
00:40:49,823 --> 00:40:50,657
Five.

554
00:40:51,241 --> 00:40:52,159
Four.

555
00:40:53,702 --> 00:40:55,078
Three.

556
00:40:55,162 --> 00:40:56,079
Two.

557
00:40:56,705 --> 00:40:57,956
One.

558
00:40:58,039 --> 00:40:59,958
Time is up.

559
00:41:00,041 --> 00:41:03,795
Chefs, take your hands off everything
and step away from the kitchen tables.

560
00:41:03,879 --> 00:41:08,008
Let me introduce the staff
who will serve the dishes in the order

561
00:41:08,091 --> 00:41:10,260
that they were completed!

562
00:41:26,985 --> 00:41:28,737
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026

563
00:41:30,697 --> 00:41:33,241
I deliberately tried
not to make it too complicated.

564
00:41:33,867 --> 00:41:36,745
Satisfying both the parents and children,

565
00:41:36,828 --> 00:41:38,496
that's what I focused on.

566
00:41:49,716 --> 00:41:54,095
Using the same taste
to bridge a 30-year generation gap

567
00:41:54,179 --> 00:41:55,222
is hard.

568
00:41:56,264 --> 00:41:59,017
So I made half the dish
for one generation,

569
00:41:59,100 --> 00:42:01,186
and the other half for the next.

570
00:42:02,020 --> 00:42:06,441
My goal was to portray
the feel of each generation on the plate.

571
00:42:08,235 --> 00:42:11,738
-The fish…
-It's not a taste I recognize.

572
00:42:12,447 --> 00:42:13,365
Is it a soup?

573
00:42:23,333 --> 00:42:24,793
I was born in Peru,

574
00:42:24,876 --> 00:42:27,254
where they eat
a lot of freshwater seafood.

575
00:42:27,337 --> 00:42:32,175
I mixed various seafood with seasonings
to evoke the feeling of an island.

576
00:42:32,259 --> 00:42:34,970
I used South American cooking techniques,

577
00:42:35,053 --> 00:42:37,847
but the roots of the dish are Korean.

578
00:42:39,766 --> 00:42:41,601
My mother is Korean.

579
00:42:59,369 --> 00:43:02,872
I didn't care too much about seafood.

580
00:43:02,956 --> 00:43:06,084
Instead, I wondered
how to get a family to talk

581
00:43:06,167 --> 00:43:08,211
as they enjoyed my food.

582
00:43:09,254 --> 00:43:10,880
That was my focus.

583
00:43:10,964 --> 00:43:13,466
The mission was about
family and communication.

584
00:43:20,765 --> 00:43:22,058
Rather than flavor and aroma,

585
00:43:22,726 --> 00:43:24,436
I focused more on texture.

586
00:43:25,520 --> 00:43:27,689
The enjoyment you get from chewing?

587
00:43:29,024 --> 00:43:30,525
Different textures

588
00:43:31,109 --> 00:43:32,277
appeal to everyone.

589
00:43:32,986 --> 00:43:36,448
Children are especially in tune with that.

590
00:43:46,374 --> 00:43:48,460
Since it's the first round,

591
00:43:48,543 --> 00:43:52,589
I first considered
what the spirit of Korean cuisine was.

592
00:43:52,672 --> 00:43:55,300
Not of a boring story
steeped in tradition,

593
00:43:55,800 --> 00:43:57,344
but the spirit of Korean cuisine…

594
00:43:58,636 --> 00:44:00,055
that we need today.

595
00:44:00,972 --> 00:44:02,599
Yes, the spirit of it.

596
00:44:02,682 --> 00:44:04,476
The essence of Korean cuisine

597
00:44:04,976 --> 00:44:06,644
is a mother's heart.

598
00:44:06,728 --> 00:44:08,897
That's where everything starts.

599
00:44:08,980 --> 00:44:10,106
My daughter…

600
00:44:10,190 --> 00:44:11,900
PARK JE-HYEON
OWNER-CHEF OF BINGSAK

601
00:44:11,983 --> 00:44:13,902
…is very ill.

602
00:44:13,985 --> 00:44:16,863
I love her more than anyone in the world.

603
00:44:19,407 --> 00:44:20,867
Out of everything I can cook,

604
00:44:20,950 --> 00:44:24,537
what could my daughter eat
that would make her smile happily?

605
00:44:26,039 --> 00:44:27,207
With that in mind…

606
00:44:27,290 --> 00:44:28,583
With that in mind…

607
00:44:30,418 --> 00:44:32,212
With that in mind, I made this dish.

608
00:44:47,435 --> 00:44:48,645
ROUND 1 SCORE CARD

609
00:44:54,109 --> 00:44:56,277
I am confident.

610
00:44:57,153 --> 00:44:58,738
Until the final round,

611
00:44:59,280 --> 00:45:02,075
I'll show you dishes that go beyond

612
00:45:02,992 --> 00:45:04,494
your expectations.

613
00:45:07,330 --> 00:45:09,666
The seventh dish of the day

614
00:45:10,875 --> 00:45:12,794
is by Exception.

615
00:45:13,878 --> 00:45:14,754
Please reveal

616
00:45:15,338 --> 00:45:16,214
your dish.

617
00:45:25,932 --> 00:45:28,393
It's chicken breast
filled with a custard-like mixture

618
00:45:29,185 --> 00:45:30,645
made with egg meringue.

619
00:45:31,646 --> 00:45:34,691
The wine-braised chicken breast is tender,

620
00:45:35,442 --> 00:45:38,111
and the custard will have expanded inside.

621
00:45:38,194 --> 00:45:41,531
It provides a simultaneous feast
for your eyes and mouth.

622
00:45:42,198 --> 00:45:44,117
I actually got the idea for this

623
00:45:44,200 --> 00:45:47,036
from a famous pastry that you all know.

624
00:45:47,120 --> 00:45:49,664
The snack you all love and enjoy.

625
00:45:51,082 --> 00:45:52,792
Yes, that's right.

626
00:45:54,794 --> 00:45:56,379
I wanted to create something

627
00:45:56,463 --> 00:45:59,883
that looks familiar,
but tastes much better.

628
00:46:01,468 --> 00:46:03,553
I slightly reshaped the chicken breast.

629
00:46:04,679 --> 00:46:06,097
I'm not sure how it turned out,

630
00:46:06,723 --> 00:46:10,226
but I shaped it to resemble
an island off the west coast.

631
00:46:10,310 --> 00:46:13,730
It's where I grew up as a child.

632
00:46:15,231 --> 00:46:18,151
The custard inside the chicken breast
consists of 12 ingredients.

633
00:46:18,234 --> 00:46:19,360
I tried to make sure

634
00:46:19,986 --> 00:46:21,779
each ingredient comes through distinctly,

635
00:46:21,863 --> 00:46:23,156
so I'd like the families

636
00:46:23,656 --> 00:46:26,951
to try and guess what they are.

637
00:46:27,702 --> 00:46:30,330
I hope this serves as a fun experience.

638
00:47:11,162 --> 00:47:12,413
This is insane.

639
00:47:12,497 --> 00:47:13,915
This is unbearable to watch.

640
00:47:13,998 --> 00:47:17,418
It's almost dinnertime
and nothing I eat will satisfy me now.

641
00:47:18,044 --> 00:47:21,256
Ms. Seon, it's our first team dinner.
Be prepared to treat us well.

642
00:47:28,930 --> 00:47:29,973
It's nice and light.

643
00:47:32,517 --> 00:47:35,270
It's time for the last dish.

644
00:47:35,353 --> 00:47:37,188
Today's final dish

645
00:47:37,272 --> 00:47:39,566
is from Famiglia.

646
00:48:15,393 --> 00:48:20,231
Dear judges, who tasted
all the chefs' amazing dishes.

647
00:48:22,066 --> 00:48:26,821
It's time for you to taste
the final dish of the first round.

648
00:48:47,967 --> 00:48:50,928
The dish prepared for you by Famiglia.

649
00:48:51,012 --> 00:48:52,138
Please reveal the dish!

650
00:49:13,743 --> 00:49:14,786
Oh, my gosh.

651
00:49:30,468 --> 00:49:31,469
Doenjang and rice.

652
00:49:32,261 --> 00:49:34,681
Abalone and finely chopped miyeokgwi.

653
00:51:10,151 --> 00:51:12,820
Doenjang is a well-known
Korean ingredient.

654
00:51:14,280 --> 00:51:16,324
It's close to yet distant from our hearts.

655
00:51:17,825 --> 00:51:20,661
Older generations
are used to the pungent smell,

656
00:51:20,745 --> 00:51:22,038
but younger generations,

657
00:51:22,622 --> 00:51:24,791
despite knowing
that doenjang is good for them,

658
00:51:24,874 --> 00:51:27,210
don't like it much because of the smell.

659
00:51:28,961 --> 00:51:31,339
It's considered good and right, but old.

660
00:51:32,048 --> 00:51:33,674
It's a lot like our parents' teachings.

661
00:51:35,301 --> 00:51:37,512
The doenjang I used
isn't commercially produced.

662
00:51:38,888 --> 00:51:42,016
It's made by an old lady in Gangjin.

663
00:51:42,099 --> 00:51:46,103
She adds a little rice bran
while the meju is fermenting.

664
00:51:46,687 --> 00:51:48,564
That way, the doenjang
retains the deep flavor

665
00:51:49,524 --> 00:51:52,360
but loses a bit of the smell
while intensifying the flavor.

666
00:51:52,944 --> 00:51:54,153
I used this doenjang

667
00:51:54,237 --> 00:51:57,031
with various vegetables
to make gangdoenjang.

668
00:51:57,114 --> 00:51:59,951
And I incorporated that
into an Italian dish.

669
00:52:00,576 --> 00:52:04,622
The doenjang helps the older generation
appreciate an unfamiliar recipe,

670
00:52:04,705 --> 00:52:09,168
while the curious younger generation
will notice the unique taste and smell.

671
00:52:10,253 --> 00:52:11,879
I hoped that would get them talking.

672
00:52:13,548 --> 00:52:14,549
Because curiosity

673
00:52:15,299 --> 00:52:16,342
is the first step

674
00:52:17,426 --> 00:52:18,761
to getting closer.

675
00:52:20,638 --> 00:52:22,849
Please take a closer look at the dish.

676
00:52:26,644 --> 00:52:30,481
The ravioli is connected
by barely visible threads.

677
00:52:31,357 --> 00:52:32,775
They represent my wish.

678
00:52:35,862 --> 00:52:37,989
We may forget in our busy everyday lives,

679
00:52:39,615 --> 00:52:40,867
but each one of us

680
00:52:41,492 --> 00:52:43,244
is connected by an invisible thread.

681
00:52:45,621 --> 00:52:48,040
A thread called longing.

682
00:52:49,625 --> 00:52:52,253
I wanted to remind you
of the importance of it.

683
00:53:00,261 --> 00:53:04,849
Chefs tend to use
Japanese white miso in Western cuisine.

684
00:53:05,850 --> 00:53:08,644
But the flavor and aroma
of our traditional doenjang

685
00:53:09,604 --> 00:53:13,441
is deeper and richer
than anything comparable.

686
00:53:14,775 --> 00:53:18,279
If its distinct smell makes it
incompatible with Western cuisine,

687
00:53:18,362 --> 00:53:19,822
then it's up to chefs like me

688
00:53:20,656 --> 00:53:23,784
to help the two communicate.

689
00:53:32,418 --> 00:53:35,046
Isn't that another way to connect

690
00:53:35,671 --> 00:53:37,173
isolated islands

691
00:53:39,258 --> 00:53:40,676
that want to get closer?

692
00:53:43,387 --> 00:53:45,806
Do you know how much deeper and richer

693
00:53:45,890 --> 00:53:48,351
our traditional doenjang is
compared to white miso?

694
00:53:49,060 --> 00:53:50,686
Saying the strong smell

695
00:53:50,770 --> 00:53:53,648
makes it incompatible
with Western cuisine is an excuse.

696
00:53:53,731 --> 00:53:55,650
What chefs like us must do

697
00:53:56,317 --> 00:53:58,069
is help them communicate.

698
00:54:01,989 --> 00:54:04,492
I will now announce
the result of the first round.

699
00:54:05,952 --> 00:54:07,787
The first chef to be eliminated is…

700
00:54:22,677 --> 00:54:23,678
What a shame.

701
00:54:24,470 --> 00:54:26,555
It's Isabel Dominguez.

702
00:54:32,687 --> 00:54:33,813
Isabel.

703
00:54:33,896 --> 00:54:37,316
You took a half-and-half approach
and served us a dish

704
00:54:37,400 --> 00:54:41,237
where you thought
the older generation would like half

705
00:54:41,320 --> 00:54:43,864
and the younger generation
would like the other half.

706
00:54:44,573 --> 00:54:47,284
We agreed that though
the food was delicious,

707
00:54:48,035 --> 00:54:50,538
it didn't adhere to the mission
that was about communication.

708
00:54:51,288 --> 00:54:55,042
It was just a way
to satisfy two different generations.

709
00:54:55,126 --> 00:54:58,546
The food was delicious,
and it was well-prepared.

710
00:54:59,463 --> 00:55:01,382
The dish itself lacked nothing.

711
00:55:01,465 --> 00:55:06,721
However, I wasn't exactly sure
if I would consider it K-food.

712
00:55:12,018 --> 00:55:12,852
Good job.

713
00:55:14,937 --> 00:55:15,938
You did well too.

714
00:55:20,651 --> 00:55:23,112
The aim of The Table: K-Chef 2026

715
00:55:23,195 --> 00:55:25,364
is to crown the best K-chef.

716
00:55:26,240 --> 00:55:27,408
What we want to see

717
00:55:27,491 --> 00:55:29,493
isn't just K-food
that's all about technique.

718
00:55:29,577 --> 00:55:31,662
We want to see K-food as a work of art.

719
00:55:35,499 --> 00:55:39,670
It's with a heavy heart that I announce
Isabel must leave the competition.

720
00:55:40,546 --> 00:55:41,630
It's a great shame.

721
00:55:41,714 --> 00:55:43,924
However, we believe this experience

722
00:55:44,008 --> 00:55:47,428
will help you become an even better chef.

723
00:55:48,137 --> 00:55:48,971
Isabel.

724
00:55:49,055 --> 00:55:53,267
Is there anything you'd like to say
before you leave the stage?

725
00:56:30,930 --> 00:56:31,764
Hola.

726
00:56:40,981 --> 00:56:43,317
I hated my mom when I was young.

727
00:56:44,360 --> 00:56:47,321
She was the typical strict Korean mom.

728
00:56:47,822 --> 00:56:49,573
I hated that, so we fought a lot.

729
00:56:52,118 --> 00:56:53,953
I wanted to hang out with my friends,

730
00:56:54,787 --> 00:56:57,206
listen to music, and dance.

731
00:56:58,582 --> 00:57:00,251
Then one day, I ran away from home.

732
00:57:01,502 --> 00:57:04,463
I left my countryside home
and went to Lima, the big city.

733
00:57:06,549 --> 00:57:08,467
I found work easily

734
00:57:09,093 --> 00:57:09,969
and I…

735
00:57:11,011 --> 00:57:13,097
I got into city life
without much of a struggle.

736
00:57:19,895 --> 00:57:22,273
But the good times didn't last.

737
00:57:22,982 --> 00:57:25,401
I was conned out of all my savings…

738
00:57:27,236 --> 00:57:28,863
and those I trusted betrayed me.

739
00:57:28,946 --> 00:57:31,657
I lost my job and got evicted too.

740
00:57:31,740 --> 00:57:35,494
I spent months like a beggar
with no work or home.

741
00:57:40,666 --> 00:57:43,544
One day, I was sitting on the curb,
too hungry and weak to stand.

742
00:57:46,046 --> 00:57:48,007
I must've looked like an actual beggar.

743
00:57:48,090 --> 00:57:50,676
Someone handed me a bill.

744
00:57:54,096 --> 00:57:56,807
It was enough to buy a hot meal.

745
00:58:01,061 --> 00:58:03,689
That's when I noticed
the sign of a Korean restaurant.

746
00:58:04,690 --> 00:58:07,693
I went in without hesitation
and ordered a kimchi jjigae.

747
00:58:14,575 --> 00:58:17,286
I ate a spoonful
as soon as I got the food.

748
00:58:23,000 --> 00:58:24,877
Tears started to flow.

749
00:58:26,670 --> 00:58:29,215
I felt my mom in the food.

750
00:58:32,384 --> 00:58:35,429
The kimchi jjigae
that my mom used to make for me.

751
00:58:37,014 --> 00:58:38,349
To me, that was my mom

752
00:58:40,643 --> 00:58:42,019
and my country Korea.

753
00:58:55,699 --> 00:58:57,910
I'm a massive BTS fan.

754
00:58:59,703 --> 00:59:03,290
I heard they'd open
a restaurant in Seoul for the winner.

755
00:59:08,087 --> 00:59:11,715
I really wanted BTS to try my food.

756
00:59:17,137 --> 00:59:19,306
The winner of the first main round

757
00:59:19,807 --> 00:59:21,058
is Famiglia!

758
00:59:21,141 --> 00:59:22,226
Oh my gosh!

759
00:59:24,687 --> 00:59:25,646
Well done.

760
00:59:28,148 --> 00:59:29,149
Thank you!

761
00:59:29,233 --> 00:59:30,317
Thank you!

762
00:59:30,401 --> 00:59:31,360
Thank you.

763
00:59:39,910 --> 00:59:40,953
Congratulations.

764
00:59:44,790 --> 00:59:45,666
Thank you.

765
00:59:48,335 --> 00:59:49,169
Yeah!

766
00:59:57,886 --> 00:59:59,263
Where's the appetizer for Table Five?

767
00:59:59,346 --> 01:00:01,056
It's almost done, Chef! One more minute!

768
01:00:01,140 --> 01:00:02,224
Okay. What about the salad?

769
01:00:02,308 --> 01:00:03,559
It's almost done, Chef.

770
01:00:03,642 --> 01:00:05,686
-Let's pick up the pace a bit.
-Yes, Chef.

771
01:00:11,525 --> 01:00:13,402
Is the mushroom risotto
for Table Six ready yet?

772
01:00:13,485 --> 01:00:15,404
It's done. I just need to slice
the button mushrooms.

773
01:00:17,990 --> 01:00:18,907
Okay.

774
01:00:19,533 --> 01:00:20,492
Wait.

775
01:00:24,538 --> 01:00:25,581
Okay. Go.

776
01:00:31,795 --> 01:00:32,963
Thank you for waiting.

777
01:00:33,047 --> 01:00:34,798
-Thank you.
-Thank you.

778
01:00:34,882 --> 01:00:36,675
-You're welcome.
-Excuse me?

779
01:00:36,759 --> 01:00:39,053
We ordered two sodas
and we're still waiting.

780
01:00:39,136 --> 01:00:41,847
-We've been waiting for ten minutes too.
-Okay.

781
01:00:44,433 --> 01:00:45,893
Oh, my goodness.

782
01:00:45,976 --> 01:00:47,394
-Can you get us a dry cloth?
-Oh dear.

783
01:00:47,478 --> 01:00:49,229
-Are you okay? Oh no.
-Yes.

784
01:00:49,313 --> 01:00:50,230
Are you okay?

785
01:00:50,314 --> 01:00:51,774
-Can you get us more napkins?
-Yes.

786
01:00:51,857 --> 01:00:52,983
Just a moment, please.

787
01:00:55,319 --> 01:00:56,153
Excuse me.

788
01:00:56,236 --> 01:00:58,113
-We ordered a steak ages ago.
-Hello?

789
01:00:58,197 --> 01:01:00,032
When can we get a menu to look at?

790
01:01:00,115 --> 01:01:01,742
We sat down over 20 minutes ago.

791
01:01:01,825 --> 01:01:04,203
-Can we get more pickles?
-Yes.

792
01:01:04,286 --> 01:01:05,537
-I need another spoon.
-Okay.

793
01:01:05,621 --> 01:01:06,997
When can we get our wine?

794
01:01:07,081 --> 01:01:08,999
-Yes, just a moment.
-How much longer must we wait?

795
01:01:09,583 --> 01:01:10,667
We need more water.

796
01:01:11,627 --> 01:01:13,670
-I'm sorry.
-We've been waiting forever.

797
01:01:36,985 --> 01:01:38,153
Do you know

798
01:01:38,237 --> 01:01:40,072
what you've done?

799
01:01:42,157 --> 01:01:43,033
Get it.

800
01:01:44,118 --> 01:01:45,911
Get it, at whatever cost.

801
01:01:49,289 --> 01:01:50,707
Do whatever it takes to get it.

802
01:01:50,791 --> 01:01:51,959
Or else…

803
01:01:54,711 --> 01:01:56,630
I won't let you get away with this.

804
01:01:56,713 --> 01:01:57,548
Yes, Chef.

805
01:01:58,549 --> 01:01:59,633
I'll go and get it.

806
01:01:59,716 --> 01:02:01,009
I'll get it somehow.

807
01:02:01,093 --> 01:02:03,137
I'm sorry. I'm really sorry.

808
01:02:29,496 --> 01:02:32,458
Because in the end,
you'll lose to me again anyway.

809
01:02:33,667 --> 01:02:35,210
Just like you did seven years ago.

810
01:02:35,294 --> 01:02:36,295
Chris K.

811
01:02:54,480 --> 01:02:56,023
The kitchen's all cleaned up.

812
01:02:57,566 --> 01:02:58,609
Gosh…

813
01:02:59,485 --> 01:03:02,070
You two did a great job
covering the dining room too.

814
01:03:08,285 --> 01:03:09,203
Oh, my back.

815
01:03:10,913 --> 01:03:14,166
Let's take these to the back
and get some rest, Auntie.

816
01:03:14,249 --> 01:03:15,584
Yes, let's do that.

817
01:03:19,922 --> 01:03:21,507
What brings you here this late, Ms. Seon?

818
01:03:22,382 --> 01:03:24,092
The chairwoman said

819
01:03:24,176 --> 01:03:27,471
she wanted to try the food
from the winner of the first main round.

820
01:03:27,554 --> 01:03:29,348
Ever since you made it to the main rounds,

821
01:03:29,431 --> 01:03:31,725
I heard it's almost impossible
to get a reservation.

822
01:03:32,851 --> 01:03:34,770
So I brought her
right around closing time.

823
01:03:36,063 --> 01:03:37,022
The chairwoman?

824
01:05:01,648 --> 01:05:02,899
If not me…

825
01:05:03,650 --> 01:05:04,776
who can do it?

826
01:05:04,860 --> 01:05:05,944
I'll send them away.

827
01:05:06,028 --> 01:05:08,155
They're guests who came to try our food.

828
01:05:08,238 --> 01:05:09,823
Are you looking for your birth parents?

829
01:05:10,532 --> 01:05:12,659
There can be many explanatory reasons.

830
01:05:12,743 --> 01:05:15,037
We just don't know which of them are true.

831
01:05:16,496 --> 01:05:18,248
What I want to know is…

832
01:05:18,790 --> 01:05:21,918
Who did they kill long ago?

833
01:05:22,002 --> 01:05:23,253
What's the relationship?

834
01:05:23,837 --> 01:05:25,547
What were you doing…

835
01:05:25,631 --> 01:05:27,424
sitting before the dead Kim Tae-seop?

836
01:05:28,133 --> 01:05:29,259
It's not yet time.

837
01:05:36,350 --> 01:05:38,352
Subtitle: Jennifer Jean Lim
